    "updated_at": "2025-07-15T23:15:42Z",
    "body": "> - That new attribute is a space-separated list of IDs pointing to the IDs from the stylesheet comments.\n\nMaybe I don't understand the idea of comment-based global `<style>` IDs correctly, but it seems to me that even if a global ID is in a comment above (or in?) the `<style>` element, the referencing algorithm would still need to resolve duplicate IDs:\n\n```markdown\n<!-- global-id: shared_styles -->\n<style>\n  p {\n    color: blue;\n  }\n</style>\n<!-- global-id: shared_styles -->\n<style>\n  p {\n    color: red;\n  }\n</style>\n<my-element>\n  <template shadowrootmode=\"open\" shadowRootAdoptedStylesheets=\"shared_styles\">\n    <p>what color am I/</p>\n  </template>\n</my-element>\n```\n\nI think this means that placing a global ID in a comment wouldn't really add anything over just using the `<style id>` attribute, which would need to be referenced by the same [purpose-specific deduping algorithm anyway](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11364#issuecomment-2986073886).\n\nPlease correct my misunderstanding.",
