    "created_at": "2026-08-30T18:43:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-08-30T18:43:50Z",
    "body": "A note from a use case that is not represented in this thread: page archiving.\n\nI maintain SingleFile, a browser extension that saves a page as one self-contained HTML file. Shadow roots are serialized as declarative shadow DOM, and adopted stylesheets are written as a `<style>` element inside each shadow root, because there is no way to reference a shared sheet. So the same CSS is parsed once per component instance when the saved file is opened.\n\nThe constraint I would like to add to the discussion is a distinction that has not come up here, because every participant so far runs code at both ends of the round trip. For an archiver the two ends are not alike:\n\n- JavaScript at serialization time is fine. I already run in the page.\n- JavaScript at deserialization time is not available at all. The saved file has the page's scripts removed, and it has to render correctly when opened years later, offline, in a browser I cannot predict.\n\nThat splits the options above cleanly. @noamr's option 2, marshalling into an intermediate representation restored by `setHTML`, cannot be used, since nothing will call `setHTML` when the file is opened. Option 1 is a string, but resolving `@import` against a same-document sheet still needs `@sheet`, which no engine implements.\n\nThe proposal at https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11364#issuecomment-3022752627 is the one that works for this case: parser-only IDs that exist during parsing and serialization and then disappear, with `shadowRootAdoptedStylesheets` on the template. A file written that way is reconstructed by the HTML parser with no script involved. The remark that \"other serializers are free to do the same\" is exactly the property I need, and I would use it.\n\nSo whatever shape this lands in, the request from archiving is narrow: the result must be understood by the HTML parser, not only by `setHTML()`. Also worth noting that this is the one context where \"use an external stylesheet\" is not an available answer, since the output is a single file by definition.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
